<Note
  phase="foundations"
  slug="what-is-a-neural-network"
  lede="A neural network is a big adjustable formula. This note builds it up from its smallest part, the neuron, to a full network, with worked numbers at every step."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why this note matters</h2>
  <p>
    Everything later in these notes is built from the idea on this page. A large language model such as GPT is a neural
    network. Attention, embeddings, fine-tuning and LoRA are all ways of arranging or adjusting the same basic part: a
    layer of numbers that multiplies its input and bends the result.
  </p>
  <p>
    Interviewers rarely ask “what is a neural network?” to an experienced candidate, but they constantly ask questions
    that depend on it: why activation functions are needed, how many parameters a layer has, what shape a tensor is
    after a layer, why weights cannot start at zero. If this page is solid, those are easy marks.
  </p>
  <p>Before reading on, watch this. It gives you the pictures; this note gives you the words and the detail.</p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aircAruvnKk"
    title="3Blue1Brown: But what is a neural network? (Deep learning, chapter 1)"
    meta="About 19 minutes. The best visual introduction there is. Watch it once fully before the rest of this note."
  />

  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Imagine deciding whether to go for a walk. You look at a few things: is it sunny, is it cold, are you tired? Each
      one matters a different amount. Sunshine pushes you towards “yes” strongly, tiredness pushes towards “no”. You
      add up all the pushes, and if the total is high enough, you go.
    </p>
    <p>
      A neuron does exactly this with numbers. A neural network is thousands or billions of these tiny deciders wired
      together, where the “how much each thing matters” numbers are not set by a person. The network finds them itself
      by looking at examples.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>Each everyday idea has an exact technical name. You need both: the first to understand, the second to speak.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Everyday idea</th><th>Technical term</th><th>Symbol</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The things you look at</td><td><span class="term">inputs</span> or features</td><td><Math tex="x_1, x_2, \dots" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>How much each one matters</td><td><span class="term">weights</span></td><td><Math tex="w_1, w_2, \dots" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your general willingness to say yes</td><td><span class="term">bias</span></td><td><Math tex="b" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The total of all the pushes</td><td><span class="term">pre-activation</span> (weighted sum)</td><td><Math tex="z" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>“Is the total high enough?”</td><td><span class="term">activation function</span></td><td><Math tex="f" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your decision</td><td>output, or <span class="term">activation</span></td><td><Math tex="a" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>All the “how much it matters” numbers</td><td><span class="term">parameters</span></td><td><Math tex="\theta" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Finding good numbers from examples</td><td><span class="term">training</span></td><td></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="example">A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Let us put real numbers on the walk decision. Each input is 1 for yes and 0 for no. Suppose these are the weights
    and the bias:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Input</th><th>Weight</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sunny</td><td>+3</td><td>Sunshine pushes strongly towards going.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cold</td><td>−2</td><td>Cold pushes against going.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tired</td><td>−4</td><td>Tiredness pushes against going even more.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bias</td><td>−1</td><td>With no information at all, you lean slightly towards staying home.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The rule: add everything up, and go if the total is above zero.</p>
  <p><strong>Day A: sunny, not cold, not tired.</strong></p>
  <Math block tex="z = (3)(1) + (-2)(0) + (-4)(0) + (-1) = 2 \;>\; 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{go}" />
  <p><strong>Day B: sunny, cold and tired.</strong></p>
  <Math block tex="z = (3)(1) + (-2)(1) + (-4)(1) + (-1) = -4 \;<\; 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{stay home}" />
  <p>
    That is a complete neuron. Notice three things. A <em>positive</em> weight makes an input argue for “yes”, a
    <em>negative</em> weight makes it argue for “no”, and the <em>size</em> of the weight is how loudly it argues. The
    bias moves the bar: with a bias of +5 you would go on almost any day; with −5 almost never.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here we chose the weights by hand. The whole point of machine learning is that we do not. We show the network many
    days along with what the right decision was, and an algorithm adjusts the weights until the outputs match. How that
    adjustment works is the subject of the next two notes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="neuron">One neuron, precisely</h2>
  <Figure n={1} caption="A single neuron: multiply each input by its weight, add them up with the bias, then pass the total through an activation function.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of one neuron with three weighted inputs, a sum and an activation function">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke" d="M95 70 L288 135" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M95 150 L280 150" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M95 230 L288 165" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-green" cx="340" cy="150" r="55"></circle>
        <path class="stroke" d="M395 150 H462" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="470" y="112" width="96" height="76" rx="6"></rect>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M488 170 H518 L548 128"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M566 150 H632" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d="M340 50 V88" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text x="70" y="77" text-anchor="middle">x₁</text>
      <text x="70" y="157" text-anchor="middle">x₂</text>
      <text x="70" y="237" text-anchor="middle">x₃</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="180" y="88">w₁</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="180" y="142">w₂</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="180" y="222">w₃</text>
      <text x="340" y="146" text-anchor="middle">w·x + b</text>
      <text class="small" x="340" y="170" text-anchor="middle">= z</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="340" y="40" text-anchor="middle">bias b</text>
      <text x="660" y="157" text-anchor="middle">a</text>
      <text class="small" x="340" y="240" text-anchor="middle">weighted sum</text>
      <text class="small" x="518" y="216" text-anchor="middle">activation f</text>
      <text class="small" x="660" y="182" text-anchor="middle">output</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="340" y="280" text-anchor="middle">blue = learned during training</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <p>A neuron does two steps.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Weighted sum.</strong> Multiply every input by its weight, add the results, add the bias. The total is
      the <span class="term">pre-activation</span>, written <Math tex="z" />. Mathematically this is a
      <span class="term">dot product</span> of the weight vector and the input vector, plus the bias.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Activation.</strong> Pass <Math tex="z" /> through a fixed function <Math tex="f" />. The result
      <Math tex="a" /> is the neuron’s output, and becomes an input to neurons in the next layer.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <Math block tex="z = w_1x_1 + w_2x_2 + \dots + w_nx_n + b = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b \qquad a = f(z)" />
  <p>
    In the walk example the activation was a hard rule: output 1 if <Math tex="z > 0" />, otherwise 0. That is called
    a <span class="term">step function</span>, and a neuron that uses it is the original
    <span class="term">perceptron</span>, invented by Frank Rosenblatt in 1958. Modern networks replace the hard step
    with smoother functions, for a reason that matters a lot: training works by nudging weights and watching whether
    the output gets slightly better. A step function is flat everywhere, so a small nudge changes nothing and gives no
    signal about which direction to move. Smooth activations have a slope, and the slope is the signal.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias, then applies a non-linear activation function. The
      weights and the bias are the learnable parameters.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="boundary">What one neuron can and cannot do</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      The walk neuron has one rule: add up the pushes, and go if the total is above zero. Think of it as a bar. Every
      day lands either above the bar (go) or below it (stay). One bar, two sides. That is the whole power of a single
      neuron: it can split the world into two halves with <em>one straight cut</em>, and nothing more.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is enough for many decisions and useless for others. This section shows one decision a single neuron can
      make and one it can never make, no matter which weights you give it.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    To draw this, use just two yes/no inputs, so that every possible situation fits on a flat chart. Put the first
    input along the bottom and the second up the side. There are only four situations: (0,0), (0,1), (1,0) and (1,1).
    Those are the four dots in each half of Fig. 2.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A decision one neuron can make: “both must be yes”.</strong> Say you only go walking when it is sunny
    <em>and</em> you have a friend free. Give both inputs a weight of 1 and set the bias to −1.5:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Sunny</th><th>Friend free</th><th>Total <Math tex="z = 1\cdot x_1 + 1\cdot x_2 - 1.5" /></th><th>Above zero?</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>−1.5</td><td>no → stay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>−0.5</td><td>no → stay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>−0.5</td><td>no → stay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>+0.5</td><td>yes → go</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    It works. Now look at <em>where</em> the total is exactly zero: every point with
    <Math tex="x_1 + x_2 = 1.5" />. On the chart those points form one straight line, the red line on the left of
    Fig. 2. Everything above and to the right of that line totals more than zero (go); everything below and to the
    left totals less (stay). The neuron has drawn a line and said “this side yes, that side no”.
  </p>
  <p>Now the names for what you just saw:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      This logic rule, “yes only when both are yes”, is called <span class="term">AND</span>. Programmers know it as
      the <code>&amp;&amp;</code> operator.
    </li>
    <li>
      The dividing line is the <span class="term">decision boundary</span>. The weights set its tilt, and the bias
      slides it without tilting it. Try bias −0.5 instead of −1.5 and the line moves so that three dots are “go”:
      that is the OR rule.
    </li>
    <li>
      Data that one straight line can split correctly is called <span class="term">linearly separable</span>.
      “Linear” here just means “straight”.
    </li>
    <li>
      With three inputs the chart is 3-D and the boundary becomes a flat sheet, called a plane. With more inputs you
      cannot draw it, but the idea is identical, and the general name is a <span class="term">hyperplane</span>.
      “Hyper” means nothing scary: it is “the same flat cut, in more dimensions”.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Figure n={2} caption="Each dot is one possible input (x₁, x₂). Filled dots should output 1. One straight line separates AND, but no single line can separate XOR.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 290" role="img" aria-label="Two plots: AND is separable by one line, XOR is not">
      {
        gates.map((g) => (
          <>
            <g class="sketch">
              <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${g.ox - 30} 210 H${g.ox + 170} M${g.ox} 240 V50`} />
              {g.points.map((p) => (
                <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={p.cx} cy={p.cy} r="11" style={p.on ? 'fill: var(--ink)' : undefined} />
              ))}
            </g>
            <text x={g.ox + 60} y="30" text-anchor="middle">
              {g.name}
            </text>
            <text class="small" x={g.ox + 178} y="216">
              x₁
            </text>
            <text class="small" x={g.ox - 8} y="42" text-anchor="end">
              x₂
            </text>
          </>
        ))
      }
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M282 194 L126 38" stroke-width="3"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M405 150 L610 150" stroke-dasharray="7 6"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M510 245 L510 55" stroke-dasharray="7 6"></path>
      </g>
      <text class="small label-red" x="170" y="275" text-anchor="middle">one line does it</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="510" y="275" text-anchor="middle">every line gets a dot wrong</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <p>
    <strong>A decision one neuron can never make: “exactly one of the two”.</strong> Picture a staircase light with
    a switch at the top and a switch at the bottom. The light is on when exactly one switch is up. Both down: off.
    Both up: off. This rule is called <span class="term">XOR</span>, short for “exclusive or”.
  </p>
  <p>
    Look at the right half of Fig. 2. The two “on” dots sit on opposite corners, (0,1) and (1,0), and the two “off”
    dots sit on the other two corners. Try to draw one straight line with both filled dots on one side and both empty
    dots on the other. You cannot. The dashed lines show two attempts; each one puts a dot on the wrong side.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do not have to trust the picture. Here is the same thing with arithmetic. Suppose some weights
    <Math tex="w_1, w_2" /> and bias <Math tex="b" /> did solve XOR. Then four things would have to be true at once:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Both off, (0,0): the total is just <Math tex="b" />, and it must be <em>at or below</em> zero.</li>
    <li>Only the second on, (0,1): <Math tex="w_2 + b" /> must be <em>above</em> zero.</li>
    <li>Only the first on, (1,0): <Math tex="w_1 + b" /> must be <em>above</em> zero.</li>
    <li>Both on, (1,1): <Math tex="w_1 + w_2 + b" /> must be <em>at or below</em> zero.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Add lines 2 and 3 together: <Math tex="w_1 + w_2 + 2b" /> is above zero. But line 4 says
    <Math tex="w_1 + w_2 + b" /> is at or below zero, and line 1 says <Math tex="b" /> is at or below zero, so their sum
    <Math tex="w_1 + w_2 + 2b" /> is also at or below zero. The same number cannot be both. So no weights exist.
    One straight cut cannot do “exactly one”.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is not a trivia point. In 1969 Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert made exactly this argument in their book
    <em>Perceptrons</em>, and interest in neural networks collapsed for years. The way out, which you will see
    <a href="#xor">worked through with numbers below</a>, is to use more than one neuron, arranged in layers, with a
    bend between the layers. The next two sections explain what that bend is and why it is needed.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="http://neuralnetworksanddeeplearning.com/chap1.html"
    title="Michael Nielsen: Neural Networks and Deep Learning, chapter 1"
    meta="Read the sections “Perceptrons” and “Sigmoid neurons”. They cover this exact step, from hard step to smooth activation, slowly and clearly."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A single neuron is a linear classifier: its decision boundary is a hyperplane, so it can only solve linearly
      separable problems. XOR is the standard counter-example, and it is why we need hidden layers with
      non-linearities.”
    </p>
    <p>
      In plain terms, that sentence says: one neuron draws one straight cut; “exactly one of two” needs more than a
      straight cut; so we stack neurons and put a bend between them.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="activation">Why the bend matters</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      With only straight rulers you can draw only straight lines, no matter how many rulers you join end to end. To
      draw a curve you need something that bends. The activation function is the bend.
    </p>
    <p>
      The obvious idea for beating XOR is “use two neurons, one after the other”. The surprise is that this does
      nothing on its own. Two straight rules in a row are still one straight rule. This section shows that with
      numbers, then names the idea.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Take two tiny neurons with one input each and <em>no</em> activation function, so each one just outputs its
    total. The first has weight 2 and bias 1. The second reads the first one’s output, with weight 3 and bias −4:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\text{first: } z_1 = 2x + 1 \qquad \text{second: } y = 3z_1 - 4" />
  <p>Now feed the first into the second and simplify:</p>
  <Math block tex="y = 3(2x + 1) - 4 = 6x + 3 - 4 = 6x - 1" />
  <p>
    The chain of two neurons is exactly the same as <em>one</em> neuron with weight 6 and bias −1. Check it: for
    <Math tex="x = 2" />, the first neuron gives 5, the second gives 11, and the single neuron gives
    <Math tex="6 \cdot 2 - 1 = 11" />. Two layers of “multiply and add” always squash down into one layer of
    “multiply and add”. Add a third layer and it squashes again. A hundred such layers are still one straight cut, and
    still cannot do XOR.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is the name for this. A rule where the output changes in steady proportion to the input, so that its graph is
    a straight line, is called <span class="term">linear</span>. The weighted sum is linear. Chaining linear rules
    gives a linear rule, which is the squashing you just saw. Anything that bends the graph is
    <span class="term">non-linear</span>, and the activation function is where the network gets its bend. Once each
    layer bends its output, the next layer combines bent pieces instead of straight ones, and bent pieces can be put
    together into any shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    For whole layers, where each neuron has many inputs, the same substitution is written with the weight tables
    you will meet in the <a href="#shapes">shapes section</a>. You can skip past this line for now and come back
    after that section; it says nothing the number example did not:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="W_2(W_1\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b}_1) + \mathbf{b}_2 = (W_2W_1)\,\mathbf{x} + (W_2\mathbf{b}_1 + \mathbf{b}_2)" />
  <p>
    Read it as: “layer two applied to layer one” equals “one layer with weights <Math tex="W_2W_1" /> and bias
    <Math tex="W_2\mathbf{b}_1 + \mathbf{b}_2" />”. Same squash, bigger numbers. This is the single most common “why”
    question about neural networks, so learn the small example well enough to write it on a whiteboard.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Without a non-linear activation, a stack of layers collapses algebraically into one linear layer, because a
      composition of linear maps is linear. The non-linearity is what makes depth useful.”
    </p>
    <p>
      “Composition of linear maps” is the interviewer’s phrase for “chaining multiply-and-add rules”. If asked to
      show it, write the two-neuron example above.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="activations">The activation functions to know</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      The activation function is the “is the total high enough?” rule from the walk example, drawn as a graph: the
      total goes in along the bottom, the neuron’s answer comes out up the side. In the walk example that graph was a
      cliff: 0 all the way, then suddenly 1 at zero. Every modern activation replaces the cliff with some kind of
      ramp. The pictures in Fig. 3 are those ramps.
    </p>
    <p>
      Why a ramp? Training works by nudging a weight a tiny bit and checking whether the answer got a tiny bit better.
      On a cliff a tiny nudge changes nothing, so there is no feedback. On a ramp every nudge moves the answer a
      little, so there is always a signal. The steepness of the ramp at a point is its <em>slope</em>. Later notes
      call the same thing the <em>gradient</em>; it is the number training relies on.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={3} caption="Three common activation functions. Each takes the weighted sum z on the horizontal axis and returns the neuron’s output." wide>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 260" role="img" aria-label="Plots of ReLU, sigmoid and GELU activation functions">
      {
        plots.map((p) => (
          <>
            <g class="sketch">
              <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${p.cx - 85} 150 H${p.cx + 85} M${p.cx} 60 V190`} />
              <path class="stroke ink-red" d={p.d} stroke-width="3" />
            </g>
            <text x={p.cx} y="38" text-anchor="middle">
              {p.name}
            </text>
            <text class="small" x={p.cx} y="230" text-anchor="middle">
              {p.formula}
            </text>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <p>Read the three pictures left to right:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>ReLU</strong> (“rectified linear unit”, said “rell-you”) is the simplest ramp: if the total is negative,
      output 0; if it is positive, pass it through unchanged. In the walk example: “a bad day counts as zero, a good
      day counts as exactly how good it is”. Its formula <Math tex="\max(0, z)" /> means “the bigger of 0 and
      <Math tex="z" />”.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sigmoid</strong> is an S-shaped ramp that squeezes any total into a number between 0 and 1. A total of
      0 gives 0.5, a big positive total gives nearly 1, a big negative total gives nearly 0. Because the output looks
      like a probability (“70% sure it is a yes”), it is used when the network must answer a yes/no question with a
      confidence.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GELU</strong> is ReLU with the corner rounded off. For clearly positive totals it is the same as ReLU;
      near zero it dips slightly below zero and curves smoothly instead of bending sharply. It is what GPT uses.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You should be able to name each function in the table below, sketch its shape, and state one strength and one
    weakness. The “slope” column matters because training multiplies slopes together across layers; the
    backpropagation note shows why.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Name</th><th>In words</th><th>Formula</th><th>Output range</th><th>Slope</th><th>Where it is used</th><th>Weakness</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Step</td>
          <td>A cliff: 0, then 1.</td>
          <td>1 if <Math tex="z>0" />, else 0</td>
          <td>0 or 1</td>
          <td>0 everywhere</td>
          <td>The 1958 perceptron</td>
          <td>No slope, so training gets no signal.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sigmoid</td>
          <td>An S-curve squeezing anything into 0…1.</td>
          <td><Math tex="\dfrac{1}{1 + e^{-z}}" /></td>
          <td>0 to 1</td>
          <td>At most 0.25</td>
          <td>Output layer for a yes/no probability; gates inside LSTMs</td>
          <td>Flat at both ends, so the signal fades (see below).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Tanh</td>
          <td>The same S-curve, stretched to −1…1.</td>
          <td><Math tex="\tanh(z)" /></td>
          <td>−1 to 1</td>
          <td>At most 1</td>
          <td>Older recurrent networks</td>
          <td>Still flat at both ends.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ReLU</td>
          <td>Negative becomes 0, positive passes through.</td>
          <td><Math tex="\max(0, z)" /></td>
          <td>0 to ∞</td>
          <td>0 or exactly 1</td>
          <td>Default for most networks</td>
          <td>A neuron stuck on the flat side outputs 0 forever (see below).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>GELU</td>
          <td>ReLU with a rounded corner.</td>
          <td><Math tex="z\,\Phi(z)" /></td>
          <td>about −0.17 to ∞</td>
          <td>Smooth, near 1 for large z</td>
          <td>GPT-2, BERT, most transformers</td>
          <td>Slightly more expensive to compute.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SiLU / Swish</td>
          <td>Another rounded ReLU, very close to GELU.</td>
          <td><Math tex="z\,\sigma(z)" /></td>
          <td>about −0.28 to ∞</td>
          <td>Smooth, near 1 for large z</td>
          <td>Inside SwiGLU in Llama-style models</td>
          <td>Same as GELU.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Three words in that table come up in interviews, so here is what each means in plain terms:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <span class="term">Saturation.</span> Look at the sigmoid picture: for totals beyond about ±4 the curve goes
      flat. A neuron out there is “saturated”: nudging its total changes its output by almost nothing, so training gets
      almost no signal from it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="term">Vanishing gradients.</span> Training passes the signal backwards through every layer,
      multiplying slopes as it goes. Sigmoid’s slope is at most 0.25, so after ten layers the signal is at most
      <Math tex="0.25^{10}" />, which is about one millionth. The early layers barely learn. ReLU’s slope of exactly 1
      on the positive side is why it replaced sigmoid inside networks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="term">Dying ReLU.</span> ReLU’s flat side has slope 0. If a neuron’s weights drift so that its total
      is negative for every input, its output is always 0 and its slope is always 0, so no training signal ever reaches
      it again. It is dead. GELU and SiLU avoid this by keeping a tiny slope on the negative side.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In the GELU formula, <Math tex="\Phi(z)" /> is the chance that a value from a standard bell curve lands below
    <Math tex="z" />. For a big positive <Math tex="z" /> that chance is nearly 1, so GELU returns nearly
    <Math tex="z" />, like ReLU. For a big negative <Math tex="z" /> it is nearly 0, so GELU returns nearly 0, also
    like ReLU. In between it blends smoothly. That is the whole trick: ReLU without the sharp corner.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="paper"
    href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.08415"
    title="Hendrycks and Gimpel: Gaussian Error Linear Units (GELUs), 2016"
    meta="Short paper. Read section 2 for the definition and Figure 1 for the comparison with ReLU. Skip the rest for now."
  />
  <p>
    One more activation deserves a mention here, although it gets its own note:
    <span class="term">softmax</span>. It is used on the <em>output</em> layer when the network must choose among many
    options, and it turns a list of scores into percentages that add up to 100%. An LLM’s final step is a softmax over
    every word-piece in its vocabulary: “the next token is ‘the’ with 31%, ‘a’ with 12%, …”.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “ReLU is the default in hidden layers because it is cheap and its gradient is 1 for positive inputs, which avoids
      the vanishing-gradient problem of sigmoid and tanh. Transformers typically use GELU, a smooth variant. Sigmoid
      and softmax are mainly used at the output to produce probabilities.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="network">From neuron to network</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      One neuron is one person making one yes/no call. Now put a row of people side by side. They all see the same
      facts about the day, but each has their own weights, so each asks a different question. One might be asking
      “is the weather good?” and so cares about sunny and cold. Another might be asking “do I have the energy?” and
      cares about tired. Then a final person listens only to that row’s answers and makes the real decision: “good
      weather and enough energy, so go”.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a neural network: rows of neurons, each row reading the row before it. The clever part is that nobody
      tells the middle row which questions to ask. Training finds questions that happen to make the final decision
      accurate.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>Now the vocabulary, one word at a time:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A row of neurons that all read the same inputs is a <span class="term">layer</span>.</li>
    <li>
      When every neuron in a layer is connected to every output of the layer before it, the layer is
      <span class="term">fully connected</span>. You will also see it called a “dense” layer or, in PyTorch, a
      “linear” layer. All three mean the same thing.
    </li>
    <li>
      A stack of fully connected layers is a <span class="term">multilayer perceptron (MLP)</span>, also called a
      <span class="term">feed-forward network</span>. “Feed-forward” means the numbers flow one way, from input to
      output, with no loops back.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Figure n={4} caption="A small network with 3 inputs, one hidden layer of 4 neurons and 2 outputs. Every line is one weight.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 340" role="img" aria-label="Fully connected network with layers of 3, 4 and 2 nodes">
      <g class="sketch">
        {edges.map((e) => <line class="stroke-soft" {...e} />)}
        {layers.map((l) => l.ys.map((y) => <circle class={`stroke ${l.fill}`} cx={l.x} cy={y} r="22" />))}
      </g>
      <text x="120" y="300" text-anchor="middle">input layer</text>
      <text x="360" y="300" text-anchor="middle">hidden layer</text>
      <text x="600" y="300" text-anchor="middle">output layer</text>
      <text class="small" x="120" y="324" text-anchor="middle">3 numbers in</text>
      <text class="small" x="360" y="324" text-anchor="middle">4 neurons</text>
      <text class="small" x="600" y="324" text-anchor="middle">2 numbers out</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="240" y="26" text-anchor="middle">3 × 4 = 12 weights</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="480" y="26" text-anchor="middle">4 × 2 = 8 weights</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <p>Fig. 4 has three columns of circles. Each column has a name:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The <strong>input layer</strong> on the left is just the facts about the day, written as a list of numbers. A
      list of numbers is called a <span class="term">vector</span>. This layer does no computing and has nothing to
      learn; it is only where the data enters.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hidden layers</strong> are the rows in the middle. They are called “hidden” because their values are
      neither the input you provide nor the answer you read out. You never look at them directly.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <strong>output layer</strong> on the right gives the answer, for example one score per possible choice.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two more words. The number of neurons in a layer is its <span class="term">width</span> (Fig. 4’s hidden layer
    has width 4). The number of layers is the network’s <span class="term">depth</span>. “Deep learning” just means
    networks with many layers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pushing one day’s numbers through the network from left to right, layer by layer, is called the
    <span class="term">forward pass</span>. It is what happens every time you use a trained model, which is called
    <span class="term">inference</span>, and it is also the first half of every training step.
  </p>
  <p>
    What do the hidden neurons end up asking? Nobody assigns them a question. During training each one drifts towards
    detecting whatever pattern helps reduce the error. In a network that looks at photos, first-layer neurons usually
    end up responding to edges, later ones to textures and shapes, and the last ones to whole objects such as “a cat’s
    ear”. Learning these in-between descriptions automatically is called
    <span class="term">representation learning</span>. Before deep learning, people wrote those descriptions by hand,
    and it was the slowest, hardest part of the job. This is the main reason deep learning took over.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="try"
    href="https://playground.tensorflow.org/"
    title="TensorFlow Playground"
    meta="Pick the circle dataset. Train with zero hidden layers and watch it fail, then add one hidden layer of 3 neurons. Hover over each neuron to see what question it learned to ask. Ten minutes here is worth an hour of reading."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A feed-forward network is a stack of fully connected layers with a non-linearity after each hidden layer. The
      hidden layers learn intermediate representations automatically; that is what representation learning means.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="xor">A hidden layer solves XOR</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Back to the staircase light, which one neuron could not handle. Here is how two helpers plus a boss manage it.
      Helper 1 counts how many switches are up (0, 1 or 2). Helper 2 only speaks up when <em>both</em> switches are up.
      The boss takes helper 1’s count and subtracts twice whatever helper 2 says. One switch up: count 1, helper 2
      silent, light on. Both up: count 2, helper 2 says 1, and 2 − 2 = 0, light off.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bend is what keeps helper 2 silent. Without it, helper 2 would say “−1” when both switches are down, and the
      trick would fall apart. Watch for that in the table.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Here is that story as a tiny network: two inputs, two hidden neurons using ReLU (negative becomes 0), and one
    output neuron with no activation:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="h_1 = \text{ReLU}(x_1 + x_2) \qquad h_2 = \text{ReLU}(x_1 + x_2 - 1) \qquad y = h_1 - 2h_2" />
  <p>
    So helper 1 has weights 1 and 1 with bias 0. Helper 2 has weights 1 and 1 with bias −1. The boss has weights 1 and
    −2. Run all four inputs through it by hand:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th><Math tex="x_1" /></th><th><Math tex="x_2" /></th><th><Math tex="h_1" /> (the count)</th><th><Math tex="h_2" /> (both on?)</th><th><Math tex="y = h_1 - 2h_2" /></th><th>XOR</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>ReLU(0) = 0</td><td>ReLU(−1) = 0</td><td>0</td><td>0 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>ReLU(1) = 1</td><td>ReLU(0) = 0</td><td>1</td><td>1 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>ReLU(1) = 1</td><td>ReLU(0) = 0</td><td>1</td><td>1 ✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>ReLU(2) = 2</td><td>ReLU(1) = 1</td><td>2 − 2 = 0</td><td>0 ✓</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    All four rows are right. Look at the first row: helper 2’s total is −1, and ReLU turns that into 0. That is the
    bend doing its job. Remove the ReLU and helper 2 would give −1, 0, 0, 1 down the column, the output column would
    become 2, 1, 1, 0, and the network would be back to being one straight cut that gets the first row wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a second way to see what happened, and it is the way to think about every hidden layer from now on. The
    hidden layer <em>re-described</em> each day using two new numbers, <Math tex="(h_1, h_2)" />, instead of the
    original <Math tex="(x_1, x_2)" />. In the original numbers, the four dots could not be split by a line. In the new
    numbers they can: the boss is a single neuron, which means a single straight cut, and it works. A hidden layer
    turns a problem that is not linearly separable into one that is. That is the job of every hidden layer: re-describe
    the data so that the next layer’s job becomes easy.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A hidden layer with a non-linearity maps the inputs into a new feature space where the classes become linearly
      separable. For XOR, two ReLU units are enough.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="shapes">Matrices and shapes</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      A <em>matrix</em> is nothing more than a table of numbers with rows and columns. Each neuron in a layer has its
      own row of weights. Write all those rows one under another and you have the layer’s weight table. The reason
      to do this is speed: a computer can multiply one table by one list of inputs in a single operation, which
      computes every neuron in the layer at once instead of one at a time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything in this section is bookkeeping: which numbers go in which row, and how big each table is. The word
      for “how many rows and columns” is the table’s <em>shape</em>. Most bugs in deep learning code are shape bugs,
      which is why interviewers ask about it.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Start small. Suppose a hidden layer has two neurons, both reading the three walk inputs (sunny, cold, tired).
    Neuron 1 is the walk neuron from the worked example, weights (3, −2, −4). Neuron 2 asks “is the weather good?”
    with weights (1, −1, 0). Stack the two rows into a table and put the inputs in a column:
  </p>
  <Math block tex={String.raw`W = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -2 & -4 \\ 1 & -1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \qquad \mathbf{x} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \qquad \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}`} />
  <p>
    The input column says: sunny, not cold, tired. “Multiply the table by the column” means: take each row, multiply
    it against the column item by item, and add up. Row 1 gives <Math tex="3\cdot1 + (-2)\cdot0 + (-4)\cdot1 = -1" />;
    row 2 gives <Math tex="1\cdot1 + (-1)\cdot0 + 0\cdot1 = 1" />. Add the biases and you get the two totals
    <Math tex="(-2, 1)" />. Row 1 is exactly the walk neuron’s calculation from before; row 2 is the second neuron’s.
    One table multiplication did both.
  </p>
  <p>Written generally, a whole layer is this one line, where <Math tex="f" /> is the activation applied to each total separately:</p>
  <Math block tex="\mathbf{a} = f(W\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b})" />
  <p>Now the shapes. For the hidden layer of Fig. 4, with 3 inputs and 4 neurons:</p>
  <Math block tex="\underbrace{W}_{4\times3}\;\underbrace{\mathbf{x}}_{3} + \underbrace{\mathbf{b}}_{4} = \underbrace{\mathbf{z}}_{4}" />
  <p>
    The table has one row per neuron (4) and one column per input (3), so its shape is “4 by 3”. In code that is
    written <code>(out_features, in_features)</code>: outputs first, inputs second. The bias has one number per
    neuron, and so does the result. Applying the activation to each of the four totals separately is called applying
    it <span class="term">element-wise</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more layer of bookkeeping, and then you are done. In practice a network never processes one day at a time. It
    takes a stack of days, say 32 of them, and processes all 32 together. That stack is a
    <span class="term">batch</span>. The input then becomes a table with one row per day and one column per input, so
    its shape is <code>(batch_size, in_features)</code>, here 32 by 3. The layer turns it into a table of shape
    <code>(batch_size, out_features)</code>, 32 by 4: one row of four answers for each day.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rule to remember: <strong>a linear layer only ever changes the last number in the shape</strong>, the one
    that counts features. Everything in front of it, like the batch, passes through untouched. You will meet this again
    in a transformer, where the input shape is <code>(batch, sequence_length, embedding_size)</code>, three numbers
    instead of two, and a linear layer still changes only the last one.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is also why deep learning needs GPUs. The work is almost entirely multiplying big tables of numbers, which
    breaks into thousands of small, independent multiply-and-add steps. A GPU is a chip built to do thousands of
    small steps at the same time.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.Linear.html"
    title="PyTorch documentation: torch.nn.Linear"
    meta="Two minutes. Check the “Shape” section and the shapes of .weight and .bias against what you just read."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A linear layer stores a weight matrix of shape out-features by in-features and a bias of size out-features. It
      maps an input of shape batch by in-features to batch by out-features, acting only on the last dimension.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="parameters">Counting parameters</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      “Parameters” is the word for every adjustable number in the network: every weight and every bias. When someone
      says a model “has 7 billion parameters”, they mean 7 billion of these numbers, each one nudged during training.
      Counting them is simple arithmetic, and being able to do it fast is a real interview skill.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Count Fig. 4 by hand. Every line between two circles is one weight. The hidden layer has 4 neurons each reading 3
    inputs, which is 3 × 4 = 12 lines, plus one bias per neuron, 4 more: 16 numbers. The output layer has 2 neurons
    each reading 4 hidden values, 4 × 2 = 8 lines, plus 2 biases: 10 numbers. The activation functions have no
    adjustable numbers at all; ReLU is the same rule forever.
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\underbrace{(3\times4 + 4)}_{\text{hidden: }16} + \underbrace{(4\times2 + 2)}_{\text{output: }10} = 26 \text{ parameters}" />
  <p>
    In general a fully connected layer with <Math tex="n" /> inputs and <Math tex="m" /> neurons has
    <Math tex="m \times n" /> weights and <Math tex="m" /> biases. Same arithmetic, bigger numbers. GPT-2 small has
    124 million parameters, counted exactly this way across all its layers. The count shows up in interviews as “how
    big is this layer?”, “how much memory does this model need?” and, later, “how many parameters does LoRA train?”.
  </p>
  <p>
    The memory question has a one-line answer. Each parameter stored as a standard 32-bit number takes 4 bytes, so a
    model’s size in memory is roughly 4 bytes × the parameter count. For 124 million parameters that is about 0.5 GB.
    Store each number in 16 bits instead and it is half that. Your laptop can hold GPT-2 small easily; a 70-billion
    parameter model at 16 bits needs 140 GB, which is why it does not fit on one consumer GPU.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not confuse parameters with <span class="term">hyperparameters</span>. Parameters are the numbers the network
    learns from data. Hyperparameters are the choices <em>you</em> make before training starts: how many layers, how
    wide, which activation, how big a step to take when nudging weights. A handy test: if training changes it, it is a
    parameter; if you set it in the config, it is a hyperparameter.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “A dense layer has in-features times out-features weights plus out-features biases. Activations have no
      parameters. At 32-bit precision each parameter costs 4 bytes, so parameter count times 4 is the rough memory
      footprint.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="depth">Why deep, not just wide</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      “Wide” means many neurons in one row. “Deep” means many rows. There is a famous result that says a single row,
      made wide enough, can imitate any smooth rule you like. So why does everyone build deep networks instead?
    </p>
    <p>
      Think of Lego. You can build any model out of 1×1 bricks alone, if you have enough of them. That is true and
      useless: a model that takes 50 ordinary bricks would take thousands of 1×1 bricks. Depth is the ordinary-bricks
      version. Early layers build small reusable pieces, later layers snap those pieces together, and the whole thing
      needs far fewer parts.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    The famous result is the <span class="term">universal approximation theorem</span>. It says: a network with just
    <em>one</em> hidden layer and a bending activation can get as close as you like to any continuous function, over
    any limited range of inputs, provided the hidden layer is wide enough. “Continuous” means no sudden jumps, and
    “as close as you like” means you pick the allowed error and the theorem promises a width that achieves it.
  </p>
  <p>That sounds as if depth is unnecessary. It is not, because of what the theorem leaves out:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not say how many neurons “wide enough” is. For many functions the number is impractically huge.</li>
    <li>It says that good weights <em>exist</em>. It does not say that training will ever find them.</li>
    <li>It says nothing about doing well on new data the network has not seen, which is the thing you actually want.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Depth fixes the first problem. A deep network builds features in a hierarchy, reusing simple features to make
    complex ones, the way a few hundred words are reused to build millions of sentences. For some functions a one-row
    network needs exponentially more neurons than a deep one to do the same job. In practice deep networks also train
    to better answers on structured data such as images and language. That is the empirical reason: people tried
    both, and deep won.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="http://neuralnetworksanddeeplearning.com/chap4.html"
    title="Michael Nielsen: A visual proof that neural nets can compute any function"
    meta="Interactive. You drag weights and watch one hidden layer build any curve out of small steps. Makes the theorem obvious without any math."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “The universal approximation theorem guarantees a single hidden layer can represent any continuous function, but
      it is an existence result: it says nothing about how many units are needed or whether training finds them. Depth
      gives the same expressive power with far fewer parameters by composing features hierarchically.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="init">How the weights start</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Before training can nudge the weights, they need starting values. The rule is: start every weight at a small
      random number. Both words matter.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Random</em>, because of a twin problem. If every neuron in a row starts with identical weights, they all
      give identical answers, so training gives them all the identical correction, so they stay identical forever. You
      paid for a hundred neurons and got one. Tiny random differences at the start let each neuron grow into its own
      question.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Small</em>, because of a whisper-and-shout problem. Each layer multiplies the numbers passing through it by
      its weights. Weights that are too big make the numbers grow layer after layer until they overflow (a shout
      that becomes a roar). Weights that are too small make them shrink to nothing (a whisper that fades). You want
      the numbers to come out of each layer about as big as they went in.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Now the names. Setting the starting values is <span class="term">initialization</span>. The twin problem is why
    you <strong>never start all weights at the same value</strong>, including zero. Using random starts to avoid it is
    called <span class="term">symmetry breaking</span>: “symmetry” here just means “all the neurons look the same”.
  </p>
  <p>
    The whisper-and-shout problem is handled by picking the <em>size</em> of the random numbers from the layer’s size.
    A neuron with 1,000 inputs adds up 1,000 products, so its weights must be smaller than those of a neuron with 10
    inputs, or its totals will be far bigger. Two standard recipes do this arithmetic for you:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <span class="term">Xavier (Glorot) initialization</span>, designed for sigmoid and tanh.
      <a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v9/glorot10a.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paper: Glorot and Bengio, 2010</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="term">He (Kaiming) initialization</span>, designed for ReLU, which zeroes half its inputs and so
      needs weights about 1.4 times larger to compensate.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.01852" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paper: He et al., 2015</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You do not need the formulas yet; PyTorch applies a sensible default when you create a layer. What you need is the
    idea: random to break the twins, scaled to the layer size to keep the signal steady. Biases are normally started at
    zero, which is fine because the random weights already make the neurons different. GPT-2 starts its weights from a
    bell curve centred on zero with a spread (standard deviation) of 0.02.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Weights are initialized randomly to break symmetry; with identical weights every neuron in a layer receives the
      same gradient and they never differentiate. The scale is chosen, with Xavier or He initialization, to keep the
      variance of activations stable across layers.”
    </p>
    <p>
      “Variance of activations stable” is the formal way of saying “the numbers neither blow up nor fade as they pass
      through the layers”.
    </p>
  </Callout>

