  <h2 id="revision">One-minute revision</h2>
  <Callout kind="revision">
    <ul>
      <li>Neuron: <Math tex="a = f(\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b)" />. Weighted sum, then activation.</li>
      <li>One neuron = one straight decision boundary. It cannot do XOR; a hidden layer can.</li>
      <li>No activation → the whole stack collapses to one linear layer.</li>
      <li>Layer: <Math tex="\mathbf{a} = f(W\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b})" />. Weight shape (out, in). Only the last dimension changes.</li>
      <li>Parameters = inputs × outputs + outputs. Memory ≈ 4 bytes each at 32-bit.</li>
      <li>Parameters are learned; hyperparameters are chosen.</li>
      <li>ReLU is the default; sigmoid saturates (slope ≤ 0.25); GELU is used in transformers.</li>
      <li>Never initialize all weights equal: symmetry. Use Xavier (tanh) or He (ReLU).</li>
      <li>Universal approximation: one hidden layer is enough in theory; depth makes it practical.</li>
      <li>Every transformer block contains this MLP: Linear → GELU → Linear.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
</Note>
